C={x["paper_id"]:x for x in json.loads((D/"interspeech-2025-fortysecond-d3-papers.json").read_text())["papers"]};P={x["paper_id"]:x for x in json.loads((D/"interspeech-2025-papers.json").read_text())["papers"]}
N={
"hannan25b_interspeech":("A speech recognizer for a device with little compute should remain useful without the memory and delay of a large model.","Accuracy, model size, latency, and energy pull in different directions, especially when training data and target hardware are limited.","Shrink a large model after training or use a tiny architecture and accept that accuracy is unrelated to the resource budget.","Design a training framework that improves lightweight ASR while measuring the accuracy-resource tradeoff directly.","The paper proposes an effective training framework for lightweight automatic speech-recognition models.","Efficiency is a constraint on the whole training-and-inference pipeline: the model must spend its limited capacity on speech distinctions that matter for the target device.","The paper reports lightweight ASR accuracy and resource results for the proposed framework.","Hardware, language, model family, data, latency measurement, and compression settings limit generalization; a benchmark model is not a deployment guarantee."),
"hermes25_interspeech":("People with congenital lip paralysis may produce acoustically similar speech using different articulatory movements, so sound alone can hide physical uniqueness.","The acoustic-to-articulatory mapping is many-to-one and compensatory; a stable sound does not imply a normal or shared production mechanism.","Classify the voice from acoustics alone or assume one acoustic pattern corresponds to one articulatory configuration.","Compare acoustic similarity with articulatory measurements in individuals with congenital lip paralysis to identify compensatory production mechanisms.","The paper studies acoustic similarities and articulatory uniqueness in speech production by individuals with congenital lip paralysis.","Speech production is an inverse problem with compensation: the same acoustic target can arise from different physical routes, so articulatory evidence changes the interpretation of similarity.","The paper reports acoustic and articulatory findings for the affected speakers and comparison conditions.","Cohort, anatomy, language, tasks, imaging or motion measures, and acoustic metrics limit clinical generalization; similarity is not a diagnosis."),
"ho25b_interspeech":("An ASR transcript should preserve laughter and speech-laugh events because they affect meaning, turn timing, and conversational analysis.","Ordinary ASR training treats non-lexical laughter as noise or deletion, while speech-laugh blends vocalization and words in ways that do not fit a simple token.","Remove laughter before recognition or fine-tune only on ordinary words and infer laughter from punctuation.","Fine-tune open-source ASR models with laughter and speech-laugh examples and evaluate how transcript enhancement changes these events.","The paper enhances open-source ASR transcripts through fine-tuning with laughter and speech-laugh.","The transcript target expands from words to interactional vocal events; the model must preserve an event boundary and its overlap with lexical speech.","The paper reports improved handling of laughter and speech-laugh in the tested ASR transcripts.","Annotation scheme, language, laughter types, model, data mixture, and transcript use case bound the result; event recognition is not a full emotion or intent analysis."),
"huang25b_interspeech":("A speech classifier can be compromised by a hidden trigger in compressed audio, causing a targeted wrong label while ordinary tests still look normal.","Compression changes the waveform and may hide or reveal trigger patterns; an attacker wants the backdoor to survive the front-end while avoiding detection.","Test clean accuracy only or assume compression destroys every malicious perturbation.","Demonstrate and analyze a backdoor attack on deep speech classification through audio compression, including its clean-task and triggered behavior.","CBA studies a backdoor attack on deep speech classification via audio compression.","The front-end becomes part of the attack surface: a transformation assumed to be harmless can carry a trigger that changes the classifier's decision.","The paper reports attack success and clean-task behavior for the tested compression-based backdoor.","Model, compression, trigger, target class, defenses, and threat model bound the claim; one attack does not establish universal vulnerability."),
"li25p_interspeech":("A multilingual ASR model should adapt efficiently to a language without overwriting what it knows about other languages.","Languages compete for shared parameters and have uneven data; full fine-tuning is costly and can cause interference or forgetting.","Fine-tune the entire model for each language or use one shared update for every language.","Use language-specific LoRA experts so a shared base retains common speech structure while small expert updates handle language-specific differences.","The paper proposes efficient multilingual ASR fine-tuning via LoRA language experts.","Parameter-efficient adaptation allocates change selectively: shared parameters carry reusable acoustics while experts absorb language-specific pronunciation and decoding behavior.","The paper reports multilingual ASR accuracy and efficiency results for LoRA language experts.","Languages, data balance, rank, routing, base model, and evaluation domains bound the claim; parameter efficiency does not guarantee fairness across languages."),
"li25s_interspeech":("A front-end speech algorithm may improve intelligibility or remove noise but leave artifacts that make the final speech sound unpleasant; a refiner should improve perceived quality without undoing the front-end benefit.","Perceptual quality is hard to predict from signal metrics, and refinement can hallucinate detail or alter speech content.","Optimize one waveform metric or apply a generic denoiser after every front-end.","Use SpeechRefiner to learn perceptual quality refinement for outputs from varied front-end algorithms and evaluate quality and faithfulness.","SpeechRefiner targets perceptual quality refinement for front-end speech algorithms.","Refinement is a second-stage correction problem: the input already contains a useful transformation, so the model must remove artifacts while preserving the first stage's content and gains.","The paper reports perceptual and signal-quality improvements for refined front-end outputs.","Front-end types, distortion, training targets, listeners, metrics, and content preservation bound the result; quality improvement is not guaranteed for unseen algorithms."),
"lin25h_interspeech":("Autoregressive speech synthesis can sound good but take too long because it generates one token after another; users need faster inference without obvious quality loss.","Speculative decoding needs a fast proposal and an accurate verifier, and speech tokens have temporal dependencies that make incorrect guesses audible.","Generate fewer tokens by skipping steps or use a smaller model and accept a quality drop.","Use a fast speculative speech model to propose multiple tokens, then verify them with the target model and keep only accepted sequences.","The paper accelerates autoregressive speech-synthesis inference with speech speculative decoding.","Speed comes from parallel verification rather than changing the target distribution blindly: accepted blocks preserve the large model's decisions while reducing serial work.","The paper reports inference-speed gains and quality behavior for speculative speech decoding.","Tokenization, proposal model, acceptance rate, hardware, speaker/style, and latency measurement bound the result; speedups vary with the workload."),
"oneata25_interspeech":("A bilingual listener may learn that a spoken word refers to one visible object, but a model trained across languages can develop a different bias about whether labels are shared or exclusive.","Visual grounding combines language, vision, and bilingual experience; correlations in a dataset can make a model prefer one interpretation without representing the underlying referent.","Measure only retrieval accuracy or assume a bilingual model follows human word-learning biases automatically.","Test mutual-exclusivity behavior in bilingual visually grounded speech models using controlled novel-object and label situations.","The paper studies mutual exclusivity bias in bilingual visually grounded speech models.","The task probes how a model allocates a new label when familiar labels already exist; its choice reveals an interaction between language-specific priors and visual evidence.","The paper reports mutual-exclusivity behavior and cross-language differences for the tested bilingual grounded models.","Languages, training data, object stimuli, model architecture, prompt/task design, and bias measure bound the claim; model behavior is not a direct account of child learning."),
}
